.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 13, 2026
.Dt MAKEFS 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm makefs
.Nd create a file system image from a directory tree
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl t Ar fs-type
.Op Fl o Ar fs-options
.Op Fl s Ar image-size
.Op Fl F Ar specfile
.Op Fl T Ar timestamp
.Ar image-file
.Ar directory | manifest
.Op Ar ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
creates a file system image in
.Ar image-file
holding the files of each
.Ar directory
and
.Ar manifest ,
without mounting anything.
A
.Ar directory
is copied whole.
A
.Ar manifest
is an
.Xr mtree 5
specification, recognized by a first line beginning
.Ql #mtree ;
every entry in it goes into the image, a file's contents read from its
.Cm contents
keyword, or from its own path, relative to the directory holding the manifest.
.Pp
Sources are merged.
A directory may come from several of them, which must agree on its mode and
owner; the first one's time is kept.
Anything else may come from only one.
Files that are hard links of each other stay one file with several names.
Regular files, directories, symbolic links and FIFOs are supported; device
nodes are not, since
.Xr devfs 5
makes
.Pa /dev .
The image holds
.Pa /lost+found
as
.Xr newfs_oxfs 8
makes it, and is marked clean.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl F Ar specfile
An
.Xr mtree 5
specification for the first
.Ar directory .
The type of each entry it names must match the file's; its mode, owner and
time replace the file's.
A directory it names that is not there is made.
.It Fl o Ar fs-options
Comma-separated options for the file system type.
For oxfs:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm label Ns = Ns Ar name
The volume label, as
.Xr newfs_oxfs 8 Fl L
sets it.
.El
.Pp
For msdos (FreeBSD's names):
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm fat_type Ns = Ns Ar 12 | 16 | 32
The FAT type; 32 by default.
.It Cm volume_label Ns = Ns Ar name
The volume label.
.It Cm volume_id Ns = Ns Ar number
The volume's serial number; by default one derived from the label and the
time, so that building twice gives the same image.
.It Cm sectors_per_cluster Ns = Ns Ar n
The cluster size, in 512-byte sectors.
.El
.Pp
FAT keeps no owners, modes or links: a file without its owner's write bit is
read-only, a hard link becomes a copy, and symbolic links and FIFOs are refused.
Names are case-preserving and unique regardless of case; one that isn't an 8.3
name gets a long name and a short alias in code page 437.
.It Fl s Ar image-size
The image's size, in bytes or with a
.Cm b
(512),
.Cm k ,
.Cm m
or
.Cm g
suffix.
Without it the image is just large enough, with a little room to spare.
.It Fl T Ar timestamp
Give every file and directory this time, in seconds since the epoch, so that
building twice gives the same image.
.It Fl t Ar fs-type
The file system type:
.Cm oxfs ,
the default, or
.Cm msdos
(FAT).
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Make an image of the base set, as the build does:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
makefs -t oxfs -o label=rootfs -s 1g -T 1792000000 root.img base.mtree
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mkimg 1 ,
.Xr msdosfs 5 ,
.Xr mtree 5 ,
.Xr fsck_msdosfs 8 ,
.Xr fsck_oxfs 8 ,
.Xr newfs_msdos 8 ,
.Xr newfs_oxfs 8
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
utility first appeared in
.Nx 1.6
and in
.Fx 8.0 .
This one is OxideBSD's own, with the same synopsis.
